const body = `<h1>Casting &amp; Integer Division</h1>

<p>
    Java has two number worlds. The <code>int</code> world: whole numbers, exact,
    fast, limited. The <code>double</code> world: decimals, approximate, huge range.
    Casting is the border crossing between them, and the border has rules. Break
    them and nothing crashes. Your numbers are just quietly wrong.
</p>

<h2>Integer Division: The Silent Chopper</h2>

<p>When <em>both</em> operands of <code>/</code> are ints, Java does integer
division: it computes the quotient and throws the remainder away.</p>

<pre><code>System.out.println(5 / 2);      // 2    (not 2.5)
System.out.println(7 / 2);      // 3    (not 3.5)
System.out.println(1 / 3);      // 0    (not 0.333...)
System.out.println(9 / 10);     // 0    (!)
System.out.println(-7 / 2);     // -3   (truncates toward zero, not -3.5)</code></pre>

<p>No rounding. No warning. <code>9 / 10</code> is zero. If your damage formula
ever does <code>damage / 10</code> with ints and damage is 9, the attack does
nothing.</p>

<p>The fix is always the same: make <em>either</em> operand a double.</p>

<pre><code>System.out.println(5 / 2.0);    // 2.5
System.out.println(5.0 / 2);    // 2.5
double d = 5;
System.out.println(d / 2);      // 2.5</code></pre>

<h2>Casting: (int) and (double)</h2>

<p>A <strong>cast</strong> converts a value from one type to another. You write the
target type in parentheses before the value.</p>

<pre><code>double price = 9.99;
int dollars = (int) price;         // 9   TRUNCATION, not rounding

int hp = 8;
double hpAsDouble = (double) hp;   // 8.0 (this one is automatic anyway)</code></pre>

<div class="callout warn">
    <strong>(int) truncates. It does not round.</strong> (int) 9.99 is 9. (int) 0.99
    is 0. The decimal part is cut off with a knife, not negotiated. If you want
    rounding, add 0.5 first for positive numbers: <code>(int)(x + 0.5)</code> rounds
    positive doubles. The exam tests the difference between truncate and round every
    single year.
</div>

<h2>Widening vs Narrowing</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Direction</th><th>Example</th><th>Allowed?</th><th>Why</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Widening (int to double)</td><td><code>double d = 5;</code></td>
        <td>Automatic</td><td>Nothing is lost. 5 becomes 5.0. Free upgrade.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Narrowing (double to int)</td><td><code>int x = 5.7;</code></td>
        <td>Compile ERROR</td><td>Information will be lost (.7 dies). Java demands
        you admit it: <code>int x = (int) 5.7;</code></td></tr>
</table>

<p>The compiler error for narrowing says "possible lossy conversion from double to
int." That is Java being a good friend. It cannot stop you, but it will not let you
do it by accident.</p>

<h2>The Casting Order Trap</h2>

<p>Parentheses decide WHEN the cast happens. This matters more than anything else
on this page:</p>

<pre><code>(int) (Math.random() * 6) + 1     // 1 to 6   cast first, then add 1
(int) (Math.random() * 6 + 1)     // 1 to 6   multiply, add, THEN cast</code></pre>

<p>Wait, those are both 1 to 6? Yes, and both correct, for different reasons. Here
is the pair that actually bites:</p>

<pre><code>(int) Math.random() * 6           // ALWAYS 0
                                  // cast binds first: (int)Math.random() is 0
                                  // then 0 * 6 is 0. Forever.</code></pre>

<p>That line compiles, runs, and produces a die that always rolls 0. No error. The
cast grabbed the random double (0.0 to 0.999) and truncated it to 0 before the
multiply ever ran. When a formula produces a suspicious constant, check cast order
first.</p>

<h2>Predict and Confirm</h2>

<div class="tinker">
    <h3>Four Mystery Lines</h3>
    <p>Write predictions first. Then run. One of these surprises almost everyone:</p>
    <pre><code>System.out.println((int) (Math.random() * 10 + 1));
System.out.println(3 + 4 + "");
System.out.println("" + 3 + 4);
System.out.println((double) 3 / 4 + (double) 5);</code></pre>
    <p>Answers: 1 to 10. "7" (int math first, then String). "34" (String grabs left
    to right). 5.75. The String ones belong to the concatenation rules in
    <a href="printing.html">Printing</a>, but they share the same lesson: Java reads
    left to right and types matter at every single step.</p>
</div>

<h2>Where Casting Lives in the Game</h2>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Random formulas:</strong> every <code>(int)(Math.random() * range) + min</code>
    you wrote in Unit 1 was casting. You have been doing this since 1.3; now you
    know why the parentheses are load-bearing.</li>
    <li><strong>Gold display:</strong> gold stored as double, shown as int.</li>
    <li><strong>Percentages:</strong> <code>hp / maxHp * 100</code> with ints is
    usually 0 (integer division!). Cast one operand: <code>(double) hp / maxHp * 100</code>.
    This is the single most common real-world cast bug in student code.</li>
    <li><strong>Math class returns doubles:</strong> <code>Math.pow</code> and
    <code>Math.sqrt</code> need a cast before they fit in an int.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Socratic Checkpoint</h2>

<div class="socratic">
    <h3>Why does Java make you write the cast for narrowing but not for widening?</h3>
    <p>Answer it in terms of information. Widening cannot lose anything, so it needs
    no ceremony. Narrowing WILL lose something, so Java requires a written
    acknowledgment. It is the programming equivalent of signing a release form.</p>
    <h3>Why doesn't integer division just round?</h3>
    <p>Rounding is a policy decision. Truncation is a mechanical one: it matches how
    the hardware divides, and it is predictable in both directions (toward zero).
    Policies belong to programmers, so Java gives you the raw behavior and lets you
    add <code>+ 0.5</code> if you want rounding.</p>
</div>

<h2>Bug Hunt</h2>

<div class="bug-hunt">
    <h3>The Percentage Bug</h3>
    <pre><code>int hp = 45, maxHp = 100;
System.out.println("Health: " + hp / maxHp * 100 + "%");</code></pre>
    <p>It prints "Health: 0%". Two fixes exist: cast hp to double before dividing,
    or multiply before dividing (<code>hp * 100 / maxHp</code>). Fix it both ways.
    Which one keeps working when maxHp is 3 and hp is 2? (The first. The second
    loses the remainder again when the product does not divide evenly. Check it.)</p>
    <h3>The Always-Zero Die</h3>
    <pre><code>int roll = (int) Math.random() * 6 + 1;</code></pre>
    <p>Predict the output range before running. Then run it 20 times and count the
    distinct values you see. Explain the bug in one sentence using the phrase
    "cast binds first."</p>
</div>

